Learning goals
By the end of the meeting, you should be able to:

find the Source, Console, Environment, Files, Terminal, and Git panes in RStudio;
run basic Chapter 1 R code and save it in a script;
clone and open a private repository as an RStudio Project;
distinguish saving, staging, committing, and pushing;
render a Quarto document to HTML; and
locate the first useful message in a failed local or GitHub check.

The central principle is: saved source files - not objects left in the current Environment - must be sufficient to reproduce the result.

Git and GitHub demonstration
The instructor will demonstrate this cycle in a disposable private repository:

clone through File → New Project → Version Control → Git;
open the .Rproj file;
replace the name placeholders;
save and inspect the diff;
commit with Add my name;
complete the first R section and make another commit;
push to GitHub; and
inspect the resulting failed Action.

The failure is intentional: only the beginning of the assignment will be complete. The useful skill is finding what remains and distinguishing an unfinished answer from a software or file-path problem.

5.1 GitHub assignment workflow
Each assignment will have a private repository created from a course template. The basic workflow is:

clone the repository to your computer;
complete the analysis and written responses in the provided Quarto file;
render the document locally;
commit and push your work to GitHub; and
check the automated tests in GitHub Actions and revise as needed.

The latest commit pushed before the deadline is the submitted version. Automated checks can verify reproducibility, required files, and many deterministic calculations; they cannot evaluate the quality of a statistical explanation or substantive argument. Passing all automated checks therefore does not guarantee full credit.
Do not make an assignment repository public or post course solutions elsewhere. If using GitHub under a name different from the one in the university roster, tell the instructor privately.

Every assignment is distributed through a private GitHub repository created for you. Accept the GitHub invitation, clone the repository through RStudio, and work inside that RStudio Project. Your ordinary cycle is:

edit and save source files;
run code and render the Quarto document;
inspect the Git diff;
commit a coherent change;
push to GitHub; and
inspect the latest GitHub Actions run.

The latest commit pushed before the deadline is the submitted version. A green automated check verifies specified mechanical and computational requirements; it does not evaluate the quality of an explanation or substantive argument.
See GitHub and RStudio for the complete first-time workflow.

